<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">NLP · Ranking · Human-in-the-loop</div><h1>Potential Talents — HR Candidate Ranking</h1><p class="section-lead">A small-data ranking project that screens noisy candidate titles, constructs a transparent proxy target and tests whether management feedback genuinely improves out-of-sample ranking quality.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<h2>Problem</h2>
<p>The supplied data contain 104 candidate records but no observed values in the intended <code>fit</code> field. The task therefore requires both defensible screening and a transparent analytical target before any ranking model can be evaluated.</p>

<h2>Screening flow</h2>
<p><strong>104 supplied rows → 52 unique-title profiles → 50 valid profiles → 49 above the semantic floor → 34 HR-relevant candidates.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Duplicate titles are collapsed to one representative profile, reducing 104 rows to 52 unique titles.</li>
<li>Two non-candidate records are removed, leaving 50 valid profiles.</li>
<li>Each valid title is compared with the locked queries “Aspiring human resources” and “Seeking human resources”; a mean raw-cosine floor of <strong>0.50</strong> removes one profile and leaves 49.</li>
<li>A deterministic HR-domain phrase check then removes 15 high-similarity false positives and retains the final 34-profile cohort: 33 direct HR profiles and one adjacent profile.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">Semantic similarity is used as a minimum relevance floor, not as a complete occupational classifier. Generic employment language can look semantically close to HR while describing a different occupation, so explicit domain evidence supplies the missing constraint.</div>

<h2>Target & modelling</h2>
<p>Because no observed fitness labels exist, the project constructs an auditable analytical target rather than pretending a ground truth is available. Occupational relevance <code>H</code> is combined with semantic relevance <code>W</code> as <code>G = (H + W) / 2</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li>100-dimensional pretrained NLPL Model 40 Word2Vec embeddings represent candidate titles and search queries.</li>
<li>The modelling pipeline is <strong>PCA → StandardScaler → Ridge regression</strong>.</li>
<li>PCA retains 95% of training-set variance and is fitted inside the modelling process.</li>
<li>Ridge regularisation is tuned with inner 4-fold CV.</li>
<li>Repeated nested cross-validation evaluates genuine out-of-sample ranking performance.</li>
<li>NDCG is primary, supported by MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Results</h2>
<p>The pre-feedback PCA–Ridge model achieved mean out-of-sample NDCG of <strong>0.9897 @4, 0.9910 @7, 0.9921 @10, 0.9947 @17 and 0.9974 @34</strong>. The W-only baseline is slightly stronger, which must be interpreted in context because W is itself one half of the constructed target G.</p>
<p>Management feedback was tested at approximately 10%, 20% and 50% review depths. Held-out NDCG changes were <strong>−0.002256, −0.005188 and −0.002668</strong> respectively. The tested feedback changed fitted rankings but did not improve generalisation enough to justify replacing the frozen pre-feedback model.</p>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<p>Retain the pre-feedback automated ranking as the current production basis, keep human review in the process, collect management disagreements in a structured form, and only promote feedback-driven retraining when future held-out evidence shows genuine improvement.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can construct an auditable target when labels are absent, combine semantic and explicit domain screening, validate a small-data ranking model honestly, and test human feedback rather than assuming it improves a model.</p>

